{"type": "messages", "messages": [{"role": "system", "content": "You are the isolated source-reviewer worker in Research World. Obey the supplied evidence and role boundary. Return one JSON object and no prose."}, {"role": "user", "content": "Review only scientific validity and inference scope. Return {\"findings\":[{\"check_id\":\"CITE-CONTEXT|SCOPE-INFLATE|ARGUMENT-CHAIN-BREAK|RESULT-ARTIFACT-MISMATCH\",\"severity\":\"info|minor|major|critical\",\"evidence\":[{\"id\":str,\"locator\":str,\"sha256\":str}],\"recommendation\":str}],\"summary\":str}. Use major only when the direction result cannot be admitted without repair.\nINPUT:\n{\"direction\": {\"title\": \"Human immune-cell perturbation platform\", \"rationale\": \"Establishes a high-fidelity ex vivo and organoid-based experimental framework to directly manipulate human immune networks, bypassing species-specific translational gaps inherent in animal models.\", \"workflow\": \"wet_lab_proposal\", \"completion_test\": \"A validated standard operating procedure for multi-parameter single-cell readouts post-perturbation, complete with dose-response matrices, viability controls, and predefined success thresholds for signaling fidelity.\", \"remaining\": \"IRB clearance, patient sample procurement, and bench-scale protocol validation.\", \"position\": 0}, \"sources\": [{\"source_id\": \"source-snapshot:e7d9528c8ec67265fdf076e446fd7cee359584dba60e41868edae3d418927d4a\", \"artifact_id\": \"artifact:70d20034e09f95f1c3cea9f4e5ecc43004fe3373bf32d1f6ff7503dfff2334c3\", \"sha256\": \"70d20034e09f95f1c3cea9f4e5ecc43004fe3373bf32d1f6ff7503dfff2334c3\", \"title\": \"Single-Cell Mass Cytometry of Differential Immune and Drug Responses Across a Human Hematopoietic Continuum - PMC\", \"url\": \"https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3273988/\", \"locator\": {\"line_start\": 1, \"line_end\": 525}, \"content\": \"## Single-Cell Mass Cytometry of Differential Immune and Drug Responses Across a Human Hematopoietic Continuum - PMC\\n\\n**Source**: https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3273988/\\n\\n---\\n\\n[Skip to main content](https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3273988/#main-content)\\n\\n**Official websites use .gov**\\nA**.gov**website belongs to an official government organization in the United States.\\n\\n**Secure .gov websites use HTTPS**\\nA**lock**() or**https://**means you've safely connected to the .gov website. Share sensitive information only on official, secure websites.\\n\\n[](https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/)\\n\\n- [Journal List](https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/journals/)\\n- [User Guide](https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/about/userguide/)\\n\\n-\\n- [](https://doi.org/10.1126/science.1198704)\\n- [](https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3273988/pdf/nihms304953.pdf)\\n-\\n-\\n-\\n\\n## PERMALINK\\n\\nAs a library, NLM provides access to scientific literature. Inclusion in an NLM database does not imply endorsement of, or agreement with, the contents by NLM\\nor the National Institutes of Health.\\nLearn more:[PMC Disclaimer](https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/about/disclaimer/)|[PMC Copyright Notice](https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/about/copyright/)\\n\\n. Author manuscript; available in PMC: 2012 Feb 7.\\n\\n*Published in final edited form as:*Science. 2011 May 6;332(6030):687–696. doi:[10.1126/science.1198704](https://doi.org/10.1126/science.1198704)\\n\\n# Single-Cell Mass Cytometry of Differential Immune and Drug Responses Across a Human Hematopoietic Continuum\\n\\n[Sean C Bendall](https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/?term=\\\"Bendall%20SC\\\"[Author])\\n\\n### Sean C Bendall\\n\\n1Baxter Laboratory in Stem Cell Biology, Department of Microbiology and Immunology, Stanford University, Stanford, CA 94305, USA\\n\\nFind articles by[Sean C Bendall](https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/?term=\\\"Bendall%20SC\\\"[Author])1,*,[Erin F\\nSimonds](https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/?term=\\\"Simonds%20EF\\\"[Author])\\n\\n### Erin F Simonds\\n\\n1Baxter Laboratory in Stem Cell Biology, Department of Microbiology and Immunology, Stanford University, Stanford, CA 94305, USA\\n\\nFind articles by[Erin F Simonds](https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/?term=\\\"Simonds%20EF\\\"[Author])1,*,[Peng\\nQiu](https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/?term=\\\"Qiu%20P\\\"[Author])\\n\\n### Peng Qiu\\n\\n2Department of Radiology, Stanford University, Stanford, CA 94305, USA\\n\\nFind articles by[Peng Qiu](https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/?term=\\\"Qiu%20P\\\"[Author])2,[El-ad D Amir](https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/?term=\\\"Amir%20EAD\\\"[Author])\\n\\n### El-ad D Amir\\n\\n3Department of Biological Sciences, Columbia University, New York, NY 10027, USA\\n\\nFind articles by[El-ad D Amir](https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/?term=\\\"Amir%20EAD\\\"[Author])3,[Peter O\\nKrutzik](https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/?term=\\\"Krutzik%20PO\\\"[Author])\\n\\n### Peter O Krutzik\\n\\n1Baxter Laboratory in Stem Cell Biology, Department of Microbiology and Immunology, Stanford University, Stanford, CA 94305, USA\\n\\nFind articles by[Peter O Krutzik](https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/?term=\\\"Krutzik%20PO\\\"[Author])1,[Rachel\\nFinck](https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/?term=\\\"Finck%20R\\\"[Author])\\n\\n### Rachel Finck\\n\\n1Baxter Laboratory in Stem Cell Biology, Department of Microbiology and Immunology, Stanford University, Stanford, CA 94305, USA\\n\\nFind articles by[Rachel Finck](https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/?term=\\\"Finck%20R\\\"[Author])1,[Robert V\\nBruggner](https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/?term=\\\"Bruggner%20RV\\\"[Author])\"}, {\"source_id\": \"source-snapshot:4d9edaa447a746aa39ddc3a645e81517c7b755fe46dcdec3c9c9db6bae3d6816\", \"artifact_id\": \"artifact:af0044a18d0ca8e3a261765db22b9675dbeeaacd2322089a3b6feff0e50765a6\", \"sha256\": \"af0044a18d0ca8e3a261765db22b9675dbeeaacd2322089a3b6feff0e50765a6\", \"title\": \"Immune Cell Modulation of Patient-Matched Organoid Drug Response in Precision Cancer Medicine Platform\", \"url\": \"https://doi.org/10.3390/cells15030259\", \"locator\": {\"line_start\": 1, \"line_end\": 2}, \"content\": \"extract_upstream_error\\nTarget returned an unsupported response.\"}, {\"source_id\": \"source-snapshot:9fe214bb4a026b0310437b977b53d875eeadf12eea2401c60fa54ee50ff5e39a\", \"artifact_id\": \"artifact:f8046f04a867c75a6c3a4809a52dc55bd000d157d1bfdaf79846b84811932785\", \"sha256\": \"f8046f04a867c75a6c3a4809a52dc55bd000d157d1bfdaf79846b84811932785\", \"title\": \"Human immune organoids to decode B cell response in healthy donors and patients with lymphoma | Nature Materials\", \"url\": \"https://www.nature.com/articles/s41563-024-02037-1\", \"locator\": {\"line_start\": 1, \"line_end\": 704}, \"content\": \"## Human immune organoids to decode B cell response in healthy donors and patients with lymphoma | Nature Materials\\n\\n**Source**: https://www.nature.com/articles/s41563-024-02037-1\\n\\n---\\n\\n[Skip to main content](https://www.nature.com/articles/s41563-024-02037-1?error=cookies_not_supported&code=d69e5d8a-9808-4353-a607-9c8e9f37a64e#content)\\n\\nThank you for visiting nature.com. You are using a browser version with limited support for CSS. To obtain the best experience, we recommend you use a more up\\nto date browser (or turn off compatibility mode in Internet Explorer). In the meantime, to ensure continued support, we are displaying the site without styles\\nand JavaScript.\\n\\n## Abstract\\n\\nAntibodies are produced when naive B cells differentiate into plasma cells within germinal centres (GCs) of lymphoid tissues. Patients with B cell lymphoma on\\neffective immunotherapies exhibit diminished antibody production, leading to higher infection rates and reduced vaccine efficacy, even after B cell recovery.\\nCurrent ex vivo models fail to sustain long-term GC reactions and effectively test B cell responses. Here we developed synthetic hydrogels mimicking the\\nlymphoid tissue microenvironment, enabling human GCs from tonsils and peripheral blood mononuclear cell-derived B cells. Immune organoids derived from\\nperipheral blood mononuclear cells maintain GC B cells and plasma cells longer than tonsil-derived ones and exhibit unique B cell programming, including GC\\ncompartments, somatic hypermutation, immunoglobulin class switching and B cell clones. Chemical inhibition of transcriptional and epigenetic processes enhances\\nplasma cell formation. While integrating polarized CXCL12 protein in a lymphoid organ-on-chip modulates GC responses in healthy donor B cells, it fails with B\\ncells derived from patients with lymphoma. Our system allows rapid, controlled modelling of immune responses and B cell disorders.\\n\\nThis is a preview of subscription content,[access via your\\ninstitution](https://wayf.springernature.com?redirect_uri=https%3A%2F%2Fwww.nature.com%2Farticles%2Fs41563-024-02037-1)\\n\\n## Access options\\n\\n[Access through your institution](https://wayf.springernature.com?redirect_uri=https%3A%2F%2Fwww.nature.com%2Farticles%2Fs41563-024-02037-1)\\n\\nAccess Nature and 54 other Nature Portfolio journals\\n\\nGet Nature+, our best-value online-access subscription\\n\\n$32.99/ 30 days\\n\\ncancel any time\\n\\n[Learn more](https://shop-amers.nature.com/products/plus/?region=US)\\n\\nSubscribe to this journal\\n\\nReceive 12 print issues and online access\\n\\n$259.00 per year\\n\\nonly $21.58 per issue\\n\\n[Learn more](https://www.nature.com/nmat/subscribe)\\n\\nBuy this article\\n- Purchase on SpringerLink\\n- Instant access to the full article PDF.\\n\\nUSD 39.95\\n\\nPrices may be subject to local taxes which are calculated during checkout\\n\\n**Fig. 1: Bioengineered hydrogel-based tonsil-derived lymphoid organoids.**\\n\\n**Fig. 2: PBMC-derived hydrogel-based organoids capture donor variability and adjuvant response.**\\n\\n**Fig. 3: Transcriptomic, BCR, clonal and SHM changes in human lymphoid organoids.**\\n\\n**Fig. 4: Simultaneous suppression of EZH2 and BCL6 in GC B cells enhances plasma cell generation.**\\n\\n**Fig. 5: Anti-CD20 immunotherapy impairs the GC formation capacity of reconstituted B cells in patients with lymphoma.**\\n\\n**Fig. 6: Lymphocyte-encapsulated tissues with switchable gradients in organs-on-chip.**\\n\\n### Similar content being viewed by others\\n\\n### [Regeneration of immunocompetent B lymphopoiesis from pluripotent stem cells guided by transcription\\nfactors](https://www.nature.com/articles/s41423-021-00805-6?fromPaywallRec=true)\\n\\nArticleOpen access10 December 2021\\n\\n### [Recreation of an antigen-driven germinal center in vitro by providing B cells with phagocytic\\nantigen](https://www.nature.com/articles/s42003-023-04807-0?fromPaywallRec=true)\\n\"}, {\"source_id\": \"source-snapshot:08861704d4587675cc21542e4702b386a05f9ec022534d0dc4e7bfc9a568731d\", \"artifact_id\": \"artifact:75e1b7dfc452d67e98ba1a00c26ae20f009951bdaf815ea385eeffda5c465aaf\", \"sha256\": \"75e1b7dfc452d67e98ba1a00c26ae20f009951bdaf815ea385eeffda5c465aaf\", \"title\": \"Frontiers | Ex vivo-generated lymphoid progenitors encompass both T cell and innate lymphoid cell fates\", \"url\": \"https://www.frontiersin.org/journals/immunology/articles/10.3389/fimmu.2025.1617707/full\", \"locator\": {\"line_start\": 1, \"line_end\": 580}, \"content\": \"## Frontiers | Ex vivo-generated lymphoid progenitors encompass both T cell and innate lymphoid cell fates\\n\\n**Source**: https://www.frontiersin.org/journals/immunology/articles/10.3389/fimmu.2025.1617707/full\\n\\n---\\n\\n## ORIGINAL RESEARCH article\\n\\nFront. Immunol., 23 July 2025\\n\\nSec. T Cell Biology\\n\\nVolume 16 - 2025 |[https://doi.org/10.3389/fimmu.2025.1617707](https://doi.org/10.3389/fimmu.2025.1617707)\\n\\n# *Ex vivo-*generated lymphoid progenitors encompass both T cell and innate lymphoid cell fates\\n\\n- [\\n\\nPG\\n\\nPierre Gaudeaux1,2†](https://loop.frontiersin.org/people/1839749)\\n- [\\n\\nJP\\n\\nJuliette Paillet1,2†](https://loop.frontiersin.org/people/2954479)\\n- [\\n\\nMA\\n\\nMonah Abou Alezz3](https://loop.frontiersin.org/people/3053927)\\n- [\\n\\nRD\\n\\nRanjita Devi Moirangthem1,2](https://loop.frontiersin.org/people/2955460)\\n-\\n\\nSC\\n\\nSara Cascione3\\n- [\\n\\nMM\\n\\nMarta Martin Corredera1,2](https://loop.frontiersin.org/people/2876570)\\n- [\\n\\nAD\\n\\nAnne-Catherine Dolens4,5](https://loop.frontiersin.org/people/1122973)\\n-\\n\\nKD\\n\\nKatrien De Mulder4,5\\n- [\\n\\nIV\\n\\nImke Velghe4,5](https://loop.frontiersin.org/people/3075834)\\n- [\\n\\nBV\\n\\nBart Vandekerckhove4,5](https://loop.frontiersin.org/people/387046)\\n- [\\n\\nML\\n\\nMarieke Lavaert4,5](https://loop.frontiersin.org/people/965927)\\n- [\\n\\nNR\\n\\nNoémie Robil6](https://loop.frontiersin.org/people/2198787)\\n- [\\n\\nAC\\n\"}], \"summaries\": {\"summaries\": [{\"source_id\": \"source-snapshot:e7d9528c8ec67265fdf076e446fd7cee359584dba60e41868edae3d418927d4a\", \"summary\": \"Metadata page for a 2011 Science publication by Bendall et al. detailing single-cell mass cytometry analysis of differential immune and drug responses across the human hematopoietic continuum.\", \"limits\": \"Lines 1-525; scope limited to publication metadata, author affiliations, and journal navigation due to preview formatting.\"}, {\"source_id\": \"source-snapshot:4d9edaa447a746aa39ddc3a645e81517c7b755fe46dcdec3c9c9db6bae3d6816\", \"summary\": \"Source retrieval failed; content consists solely of an extraction error indicating an unsupported response from the target server.\", \"limits\": \"Lines 1-2; no substantive scientific data available for summarization.\"}, {\"source_id\": \"source-snapshot:9fe214bb4a026b0310437b977b53d875eeadf12eea2401c60fa54ee50ff5e39a\", \"summary\": \"Introduces synthetic hydrogel-based immune organoids that replicate lymphoid microenvironments to sustain long-term germinal center reactions. Demonstrates that peripheral blood-derived organoids outperform tonsil-derived ones in maintaining B cell programming and plasma cell generation, which can be enhanced via transcriptional/epigenetic inhibition. Highlights differential responses to immunotherapies and microenvironmental cues between healthy donors and lymphoma patients, establishing a controlled platform for modeling B cell biology and disorders.\", \"limits\": \"Lines 1-704; scope restricted to abstract and figure captions due to subscription preview status.\"}, {\"source_id\": \"source-snapshot:08861704d4587675cc21542e4702b386a05f9ec022534d0dc4e7bfc9a568731d\", \"summary\": \"Metadata page for a 2025 Frontiers in Immunology research article investigating ex vivo-generated lymphoid progenitors and their dual differentiation potential into T cells and innate lymphoid cells.\", \"limits\": \"Lines 1-580; scope limited to publication header, author list, and journal navigation elements.\"}]}}"}]}
{"type": "model_output", "text": "{\n  \"findings\": [\n    {\n      \"check_id\": \"CITE-CONTEXT\",\n      \"severity\": \"major\",\n      \"evidence\": [\n        {\"id\": \"source-snapshot:e7d9528c8ec67265fdf076e446fd7cee359584dba60e41868edae3d418927d4a\", \"locator\": {\"line_start\": 1, \"line_end\": 525}, \"sha256\": \"70d20034e09f95f1c3cea9f4e5ecc43004fe3373bf32d1f6ff7503dfff2334c3\"},\n        {\"id\": \"source-snapshot:4d9edaa447a746aa39ddc3a645e81517c7b755fe46dcdec3c9c9db6bae3d6816\", \"locator\": {\"line_start\": 1, \"line_end\": 2}, \"sha256\": \"af0044a18d0ca8e3a261765db22b9675dbeeaacd2322089a3b6feff0e50765a6\"},\n        {\"id\": \"source-snapshot:9fe214bb4a026b0310437b977b53d875eeadf12eea2401c60fa54ee50ff5e39a\", \"locator\": {\"line_start\": 1, \"line_end\": 704}, \"sha256\": \"f8046f04a867c75a6c3a4809a52dc55bd000d157d1bfdaf79846b84811932785\"},\n        {\"id\": \"source-snapshot:08861704d4587675cc21542e4702b386a05f9ec022534d0dc4e7bfc9a568731d\", \"locator\": {\"line_start\": 1, \"line_end\": 580}, \"sha256\": \"75e1b7dfc452d67e98ba1a00c26ae20f009951bdaf815ea385eeffda5465aaf\"}\n      ],\n      \"recommendation\": \"Retrieve full-text versions or substitute with accessible primary literature detailing wet-lab SOPs, dose-response validation, and multi-parameter single-cell readout protocols to substantiate the proposed framework.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"check_id\": \"SCOPE-INFLATE\",\n      \"severity\": \"major\",\n      \"evidence\": [\n        {\"id\": \"source-snapshot:e7d9528c8ec67265fdf076e446fd7cee359584dba60e41868edae3d418927d4a\", \"locator\": {\"line_start\": 1, \"line_end\": 525}, \"sha256\": \"70d20034e09f95f1c3cea9f4e5ecc43004fe3373bf32d1f6ff7503dfff2334c3\"},\n        {\"id\": \"source-snapshot:9fe214bb4a026b0310437b977b53d875eeadf12eea2401c60fa54ee50ff5e39a\", \"locator\": {\"line_start\": 1, \"line_end\": 704}, \"sha256\": \"f8046f04a867c75a6c3a4809a52dc55bd000d157d1bfdaf79846b84811932785\"},\n        {\"id\": \"source-snapshot:08861704d4587675cc21542e4702b386a05f9ec022534d0dc4e7bfc9a568731d\", \"locator\": {\"line_start\": 1, \"line_end\": 580}, \"sha256\": \"75e1b7dfc452d67e98ba1a00c26ae20f009951bdaf815ea385eeffda5465aaf\"}\n      ],\n      \"recommendation\": \"Narrow the directional scope to align with available evidence (e.g., B-cell lymphoid organoids and mass cytometry profiling) or acquire broader immunological perturbation data before claiming comprehensive immune network manipulation.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"check_id\": \"RESULT-ARTIFACT-MISMATCH\",\n      \"severity\": \"major\",\n      \"evidence\": [\n        {\"id\": \"source-snapshot:e7d9528c8ec67265fdf076e446fd7cee359584dba60e41868edae3d418927d4a\", \"locator\": {\"line_start\": 1, \"line_end\": 525}, \"sha256\": \"70d20034e09f95f1c3cea9f4e5ecc43004fe3373bf32d1f6ff7503dfff2334c3\"},\n        {\"id\": \"source-snapshot:4d9edaa447a746aa39ddc3a645e81517c7b755fe46dcdec3c9c9db6bae3d6816\", \"locator\": {\"line_start\": 1, \"line_end\": 2}, \"sha256\": \"af0044a18d0ca8e3a261765db22b9675dbeeaacd2322089a3b6feff0e50765a6\"},\n        {\"id\": \"source-snapshot:9fe214bb4a026b0310437b977b53d875eeadf12eea2401c60fa54ee50ff5e39a\", \"locator\": {\"line_start\": 1, \"line_end\": 704}, \"sha256\": \"f8046f04a867c75a6c3a4809a52dc55bd000d157d1bfdaf79846b84811932785\"},\n        {\"id\": \"source-snapshot:08861704d4587675cc21542e4702b386a05f9ec022534d0dc4e7bfc9a568731d\", \"locator\": {\"line_start\": 1, \"line_end\": 580}, \"sha256\": \"75e1b7dfc452d67e98ba1a00c26ae20f009951bdaf815ea385eeffda5465aaf\"}\n      ],\n      \"recommendation\": \"Develop and document the required SOP, dose-response matrices, and viability controls internally prior to submission, as current sources provide zero procedural validation or threshold definitions.\"\n    }\n  ],\n  \"summary\": \"The proposed human immune-cell perturbation platform relies on fragmented, inaccessible, or erroneous source material. Available evidence consists primarily of publication metadata, a single extraction failure, and a paywalled abstract focusing narrowly on B-cell organoids. Consequently, the rationale overstates the breadth of applicable data, and the specified completion test (validated SOP with dose-response matrices and signaling thresholds) is entirely unsupported. Substantive full-text literature or internal experimental documentation is required to validate the workflow and adjust the scope accordingly.\"\n}", "tool_calls": [], "usage": {"prompt_tokens": 4700, "total_tokens": 13646, "completion_tokens": 8946, "completion_tokens_details": {"reasoning_tokens": 6950, "text_tokens": 8946}}, "model": "qwen3.7-flash"}
{"type": "attempt_input", "content": {"input": {"direction": {"title": "Human immune-cell perturbation platform", "rationale": "Establishes a high-fidelity ex vivo and organoid-based experimental framework to directly manipulate human immune networks, bypassing species-specific translational gaps inherent in animal models.", "workflow": "wet_lab_proposal", "completion_test": "A validated standard operating procedure for multi-parameter single-cell readouts post-perturbation, complete with dose-response matrices, viability controls, and predefined success thresholds for signaling fidelity.", "remaining": "IRB clearance, patient sample procurement, and bench-scale protocol validation.", "position": 0}, "sources": [{"source_id": "source-snapshot:e7d9528c8ec67265fdf076e446fd7cee359584dba60e41868edae3d418927d4a", "artifact_id": "artifact:70d20034e09f95f1c3cea9f4e5ecc43004fe3373bf32d1f6ff7503dfff2334c3", "sha256": "70d20034e09f95f1c3cea9f4e5ecc43004fe3373bf32d1f6ff7503dfff2334c3", "title": "Single-Cell Mass Cytometry of Differential Immune and Drug Responses Across a Human Hematopoietic Continuum - PMC", "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3273988/", "locator": {"line_start": 1, "line_end": 525}, "content": "## Single-Cell Mass Cytometry of Differential Immune and Drug Responses Across a Human Hematopoietic Continuum - PMC\n\n**Source**: https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3273988/\n\n---\n\n[Skip to main content](https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3273988/#main-content)\n\n**Official websites use .gov**\nA**.gov**website belongs to an official government organization in the United States.\n\n**Secure .gov websites use HTTPS**\nA**lock**() or**https://**means you've safely connected to the .gov website. Share sensitive information only on official, secure websites.\n\n[](https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/)\n\n- [Journal List](https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/journals/)\n- [User Guide](https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/about/userguide/)\n\n-\n- [](https://doi.org/10.1126/science.1198704)\n- [](https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3273988/pdf/nihms304953.pdf)\n-\n-\n-\n\n## PERMALINK\n\nAs a library, NLM provides access to scientific literature. Inclusion in an NLM database does not imply endorsement of, or agreement with, the contents by NLM\nor the National Institutes of Health.\nLearn more:[PMC Disclaimer](https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/about/disclaimer/)|[PMC Copyright Notice](https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/about/copyright/)\n\n. Author manuscript; available in PMC: 2012 Feb 7.\n\n*Published in final edited form as:*Science. 2011 May 6;332(6030):687–696. doi:[10.1126/science.1198704](https://doi.org/10.1126/science.1198704)\n\n# Single-Cell Mass Cytometry of Differential Immune and Drug Responses Across a Human Hematopoietic Continuum\n\n[Sean C Bendall](https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/?term=\"Bendall%20SC\"[Author])\n\n### Sean C Bendall\n\n1Baxter Laboratory in Stem Cell Biology, Department of Microbiology and Immunology, Stanford University, Stanford, CA 94305, USA\n\nFind articles by[Sean C Bendall](https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/?term=\"Bendall%20SC\"[Author])1,*,[Erin F\nSimonds](https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/?term=\"Simonds%20EF\"[Author])\n\n### Erin F Simonds\n\n1Baxter Laboratory in Stem Cell Biology, Department of Microbiology and Immunology, Stanford University, Stanford, CA 94305, USA\n\nFind articles by[Erin F Simonds](https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/?term=\"Simonds%20EF\"[Author])1,*,[Peng\nQiu](https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/?term=\"Qiu%20P\"[Author])\n\n### Peng Qiu\n\n2Department of Radiology, Stanford University, Stanford, CA 94305, USA\n\nFind articles by[Peng Qiu](https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/?term=\"Qiu%20P\"[Author])2,[El-ad D Amir](https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/?term=\"Amir%20EAD\"[Author])\n\n### El-ad D Amir\n\n3Department of Biological Sciences, Columbia University, New York, NY 10027, USA\n\nFind articles by[El-ad D Amir](https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/?term=\"Amir%20EAD\"[Author])3,[Peter O\nKrutzik](https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/?term=\"Krutzik%20PO\"[Author])\n\n### Peter O Krutzik\n\n1Baxter Laboratory in Stem Cell Biology, Department of Microbiology and Immunology, Stanford University, Stanford, CA 94305, USA\n\nFind articles by[Peter O Krutzik](https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/?term=\"Krutzik%20PO\"[Author])1,[Rachel\nFinck](https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/?term=\"Finck%20R\"[Author])\n\n### Rachel Finck\n\n1Baxter Laboratory in Stem Cell Biology, Department of Microbiology and Immunology, Stanford University, Stanford, CA 94305, USA\n\nFind articles by[Rachel Finck](https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/?term=\"Finck%20R\"[Author])1,[Robert V\nBruggner](https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/?term=\"Bruggner%20RV\"[Author])"}, {"source_id": "source-snapshot:4d9edaa447a746aa39ddc3a645e81517c7b755fe46dcdec3c9c9db6bae3d6816", "artifact_id": "artifact:af0044a18d0ca8e3a261765db22b9675dbeeaacd2322089a3b6feff0e50765a6", "sha256": "af0044a18d0ca8e3a261765db22b9675dbeeaacd2322089a3b6feff0e50765a6", "title": "Immune Cell Modulation of Patient-Matched Organoid Drug Response in Precision Cancer Medicine Platform", "url": "https://doi.org/10.3390/cells15030259", "locator": {"line_start": 1, "line_end": 2}, "content": "extract_upstream_error\nTarget returned an unsupported response."}, {"source_id": "source-snapshot:9fe214bb4a026b0310437b977b53d875eeadf12eea2401c60fa54ee50ff5e39a", "artifact_id": "artifact:f8046f04a867c75a6c3a4809a52dc55bd000d157d1bfdaf79846b84811932785", "sha256": "f8046f04a867c75a6c3a4809a52dc55bd000d157d1bfdaf79846b84811932785", "title": "Human immune organoids to decode B cell response in healthy donors and patients with lymphoma | Nature Materials", "url": "https://www.nature.com/articles/s41563-024-02037-1", "locator": {"line_start": 1, "line_end": 704}, "content": "## Human immune organoids to decode B cell response in healthy donors and patients with lymphoma | Nature Materials\n\n**Source**: https://www.nature.com/articles/s41563-024-02037-1\n\n---\n\n[Skip to main content](https://www.nature.com/articles/s41563-024-02037-1?error=cookies_not_supported&code=d69e5d8a-9808-4353-a607-9c8e9f37a64e#content)\n\nThank you for visiting nature.com. You are using a browser version with limited support for CSS. To obtain the best experience, we recommend you use a more up\nto date browser (or turn off compatibility mode in Internet Explorer). In the meantime, to ensure continued support, we are displaying the site without styles\nand JavaScript.\n\n## Abstract\n\nAntibodies are produced when naive B cells differentiate into plasma cells within germinal centres (GCs) of lymphoid tissues. Patients with B cell lymphoma on\neffective immunotherapies exhibit diminished antibody production, leading to higher infection rates and reduced vaccine efficacy, even after B cell recovery.\nCurrent ex vivo models fail to sustain long-term GC reactions and effectively test B cell responses. Here we developed synthetic hydrogels mimicking the\nlymphoid tissue microenvironment, enabling human GCs from tonsils and peripheral blood mononuclear cell-derived B cells. Immune organoids derived from\nperipheral blood mononuclear cells maintain GC B cells and plasma cells longer than tonsil-derived ones and exhibit unique B cell programming, including GC\ncompartments, somatic hypermutation, immunoglobulin class switching and B cell clones. Chemical inhibition of transcriptional and epigenetic processes enhances\nplasma cell formation. While integrating polarized CXCL12 protein in a lymphoid organ-on-chip modulates GC responses in healthy donor B cells, it fails with B\ncells derived from patients with lymphoma. Our system allows rapid, controlled modelling of immune responses and B cell disorders.\n\nThis is a preview of subscription content,[access via your\ninstitution](https://wayf.springernature.com?redirect_uri=https%3A%2F%2Fwww.nature.com%2Farticles%2Fs41563-024-02037-1)\n\n## Access options\n\n[Access through your institution](https://wayf.springernature.com?redirect_uri=https%3A%2F%2Fwww.nature.com%2Farticles%2Fs41563-024-02037-1)\n\nAccess Nature and 54 other Nature Portfolio journals\n\nGet Nature+, our best-value online-access subscription\n\n$32.99/ 30 days\n\ncancel any time\n\n[Learn more](https://shop-amers.nature.com/products/plus/?region=US)\n\nSubscribe to this journal\n\nReceive 12 print issues and online access\n\n$259.00 per year\n\nonly $21.58 per issue\n\n[Learn more](https://www.nature.com/nmat/subscribe)\n\nBuy this article\n- Purchase on SpringerLink\n- Instant access to the full article PDF.\n\nUSD 39.95\n\nPrices may be subject to local taxes which are calculated during checkout\n\n**Fig. 1: Bioengineered hydrogel-based tonsil-derived lymphoid organoids.**\n\n**Fig. 2: PBMC-derived hydrogel-based organoids capture donor variability and adjuvant response.**\n\n**Fig. 3: Transcriptomic, BCR, clonal and SHM changes in human lymphoid organoids.**\n\n**Fig. 4: Simultaneous suppression of EZH2 and BCL6 in GC B cells enhances plasma cell generation.**\n\n**Fig. 5: Anti-CD20 immunotherapy impairs the GC formation capacity of reconstituted B cells in patients with lymphoma.**\n\n**Fig. 6: Lymphocyte-encapsulated tissues with switchable gradients in organs-on-chip.**\n\n### Similar content being viewed by others\n\n### [Regeneration of immunocompetent B lymphopoiesis from pluripotent stem cells guided by transcription\nfactors](https://www.nature.com/articles/s41423-021-00805-6?fromPaywallRec=true)\n\nArticleOpen access10 December 2021\n\n### [Recreation of an antigen-driven germinal center in vitro by providing B cells with phagocytic\nantigen](https://www.nature.com/articles/s42003-023-04807-0?fromPaywallRec=true)\n"}, {"source_id": "source-snapshot:08861704d4587675cc21542e4702b386a05f9ec022534d0dc4e7bfc9a568731d", "artifact_id": "artifact:75e1b7dfc452d67e98ba1a00c26ae20f009951bdaf815ea385eeffda5c465aaf", "sha256": "75e1b7dfc452d67e98ba1a00c26ae20f009951bdaf815ea385eeffda5c465aaf", "title": "Frontiers | Ex vivo-generated lymphoid progenitors encompass both T cell and innate lymphoid cell fates", "url": "https://www.frontiersin.org/journals/immunology/articles/10.3389/fimmu.2025.1617707/full", "locator": {"line_start": 1, "line_end": 580}, "content": "## Frontiers | Ex vivo-generated lymphoid progenitors encompass both T cell and innate lymphoid cell fates\n\n**Source**: https://www.frontiersin.org/journals/immunology/articles/10.3389/fimmu.2025.1617707/full\n\n---\n\n## ORIGINAL RESEARCH article\n\nFront. Immunol., 23 July 2025\n\nSec. T Cell Biology\n\nVolume 16 - 2025 |[https://doi.org/10.3389/fimmu.2025.1617707](https://doi.org/10.3389/fimmu.2025.1617707)\n\n# *Ex vivo-*generated lymphoid progenitors encompass both T cell and innate lymphoid cell fates\n\n- [\n\nPG\n\nPierre Gaudeaux1,2†](https://loop.frontiersin.org/people/1839749)\n- [\n\nJP\n\nJuliette Paillet1,2†](https://loop.frontiersin.org/people/2954479)\n- [\n\nMA\n\nMonah Abou Alezz3](https://loop.frontiersin.org/people/3053927)\n- [\n\nRD\n\nRanjita Devi Moirangthem1,2](https://loop.frontiersin.org/people/2955460)\n-\n\nSC\n\nSara Cascione3\n- [\n\nMM\n\nMarta Martin Corredera1,2](https://loop.frontiersin.org/people/2876570)\n- [\n\nAD\n\nAnne-Catherine Dolens4,5](https://loop.frontiersin.org/people/1122973)\n-\n\nKD\n\nKatrien De Mulder4,5\n- [\n\nIV\n\nImke Velghe4,5](https://loop.frontiersin.org/people/3075834)\n- [\n\nBV\n\nBart Vandekerckhove4,5](https://loop.frontiersin.org/people/387046)\n- [\n\nML\n\nMarieke Lavaert4,5](https://loop.frontiersin.org/people/965927)\n- [\n\nNR\n\nNoémie Robil6](https://loop.frontiersin.org/people/2198787)\n- [\n\nAC\n"}], "summaries": {"summaries": [{"source_id": "source-snapshot:e7d9528c8ec67265fdf076e446fd7cee359584dba60e41868edae3d418927d4a", "summary": "Metadata page for a 2011 Science publication by Bendall et al. detailing single-cell mass cytometry analysis of differential immune and drug responses across the human hematopoietic continuum.", "limits": "Lines 1-525; scope limited to publication metadata, author affiliations, and journal navigation due to preview formatting."}, {"source_id": "source-snapshot:4d9edaa447a746aa39ddc3a645e81517c7b755fe46dcdec3c9c9db6bae3d6816", "summary": "Source retrieval failed; content consists solely of an extraction error indicating an unsupported response from the target server.", "limits": "Lines 1-2; no substantive scientific data available for summarization."}, {"source_id": "source-snapshot:9fe214bb4a026b0310437b977b53d875eeadf12eea2401c60fa54ee50ff5e39a", "summary": "Introduces synthetic hydrogel-based immune organoids that replicate lymphoid microenvironments to sustain long-term germinal center reactions. Demonstrates that peripheral blood-derived organoids outperform tonsil-derived ones in maintaining B cell programming and plasma cell generation, which can be enhanced via transcriptional/epigenetic inhibition. Highlights differential responses to immunotherapies and microenvironmental cues between healthy donors and lymphoma patients, establishing a controlled platform for modeling B cell biology and disorders.", "limits": "Lines 1-704; scope restricted to abstract and figure captions due to subscription preview status."}, {"source_id": "source-snapshot:08861704d4587675cc21542e4702b386a05f9ec022534d0dc4e7bfc9a568731d", "summary": "Metadata page for a 2025 Frontiers in Immunology research article investigating ex vivo-generated lymphoid progenitors and their dual differentiation potential into T cells and innate lymphoid cells.", "limits": "Lines 1-580; scope limited to publication header, author list, and journal navigation elements."}]}}}}
{"type": "attempt_output", "content": {"output": {"findings": [{"check_id": "CITE-CONTEXT", "severity": "major", "evidence": [{"id": "source-snapshot:e7d9528c8ec67265fdf076e446fd7cee359584dba60e41868edae3d418927d4a", "locator": {"line_start": 1, "line_end": 525}, "sha256": "70d20034e09f95f1c3cea9f4e5ecc43004fe3373bf32d1f6ff7503dfff2334c3"}, {"id": "source-snapshot:4d9edaa447a746aa39ddc3a645e81517c7b755fe46dcdec3c9c9db6bae3d6816", "locator": {"line_start": 1, "line_end": 2}, "sha256": "af0044a18d0ca8e3a261765db22b9675dbeeaacd2322089a3b6feff0e50765a6"}, {"id": "source-snapshot:9fe214bb4a026b0310437b977b53d875eeadf12eea2401c60fa54ee50ff5e39a", "locator": {"line_start": 1, "line_end": 704}, "sha256": "f8046f04a867c75a6c3a4809a52dc55bd000d157d1bfdaf79846b84811932785"}, {"id": "source-snapshot:08861704d4587675cc21542e4702b386a05f9ec022534d0dc4e7bfc9a568731d", "locator": {"line_start": 1, "line_end": 580}, "sha256": "75e1b7dfc452d67e98ba1a00c26ae20f009951bdaf815ea385eeffda5465aaf"}], "recommendation": "Retrieve full-text versions or substitute with accessible primary literature detailing wet-lab SOPs, dose-response validation, and multi-parameter single-cell readout protocols to substantiate the proposed framework."}, {"check_id": "SCOPE-INFLATE", "severity": "major", "evidence": [{"id": "source-snapshot:e7d9528c8ec67265fdf076e446fd7cee359584dba60e41868edae3d418927d4a", "locator": {"line_start": 1, "line_end": 525}, "sha256": "70d20034e09f95f1c3cea9f4e5ecc43004fe3373bf32d1f6ff7503dfff2334c3"}, {"id": "source-snapshot:9fe214bb4a026b0310437b977b53d875eeadf12eea2401c60fa54ee50ff5e39a", "locator": {"line_start": 1, "line_end": 704}, "sha256": "f8046f04a867c75a6c3a4809a52dc55bd000d157d1bfdaf79846b84811932785"}, {"id": "source-snapshot:08861704d4587675cc21542e4702b386a05f9ec022534d0dc4e7bfc9a568731d", "locator": {"line_start": 1, "line_end": 580}, "sha256": "75e1b7dfc452d67e98ba1a00c26ae20f009951bdaf815ea385eeffda5465aaf"}], "recommendation": "Narrow the directional scope to align with available evidence (e.g., B-cell lymphoid organoids and mass cytometry profiling) or acquire broader immunological perturbation data before claiming comprehensive immune network manipulation."}, {"check_id": "RESULT-ARTIFACT-MISMATCH", "severity": "major", "evidence": [{"id": "source-snapshot:e7d9528c8ec67265fdf076e446fd7cee359584dba60e41868edae3d418927d4a", "locator": {"line_start": 1, "line_end": 525}, "sha256": "70d20034e09f95f1c3cea9f4e5ecc43004fe3373bf32d1f6ff7503dfff2334c3"}, {"id": "source-snapshot:4d9edaa447a746aa39ddc3a645e81517c7b755fe46dcdec3c9c9db6bae3d6816", "locator": {"line_start": 1, "line_end": 2}, "sha256": "af0044a18d0ca8e3a261765db22b9675dbeeaacd2322089a3b6feff0e50765a6"}, {"id": "source-snapshot:9fe214bb4a026b0310437b977b53d875eeadf12eea2401c60fa54ee50ff5e39a", "locator": {"line_start": 1, "line_end": 704}, "sha256": "f8046f04a867c75a6c3a4809a52dc55bd000d157d1bfdaf79846b84811932785"}, {"id": "source-snapshot:08861704d4587675cc21542e4702b386a05f9ec022534d0dc4e7bfc9a568731d", "locator": {"line_start": 1, "line_end": 580}, "sha256": "75e1b7dfc452d67e98ba1a00c26ae20f009951bdaf815ea385eeffda5465aaf"}], "recommendation": "Develop and document the required SOP, dose-response matrices, and viability controls internally prior to submission, as current sources provide zero procedural validation or threshold definitions."}], "summary": "The proposed human immune-cell perturbation platform relies on fragmented, inaccessible, or erroneous source material. Available evidence consists primarily of publication metadata, a single extraction failure, and a paywalled abstract focusing narrowly on B-cell organoids. Consequently, the rationale overstates the breadth of applicable data, and the specified completion test (validated SOP with dose-response matrices and signaling thresholds) is entirely unsupported. Substantive full-text literature or internal experimental documentation is required to validate the workflow and adjust the scope accordingly."}, "tool_receipts": []}}
